{"page":438,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.37889,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"424 Chapter 7: Integrals and Transcendental Functions"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.09123,"w":0.58894,"h":0.03263,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The function ln-1 x is also denoted by exp x. We now show that ln-1 x = exp x is an expo- nential function with base e."},{"x":0.20746,"y":0.12363,"w":0.05233,"h":0.01256,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = ln−1x"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.12573,"w":0.59303,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The number e was defined to satisfy the equation ln (e) = 1, so e = exp (1). We can raise the number e to a rational power r using algebra:"},{"x":0.21236,"y":0.1362,"w":0.04252,"h":0.0211,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"or x = ln y"},{"x":0.42479,"y":0.16346,"w":0.15486,"h":0.02238,"fs":0.01918,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"e2 = e # e, e-2 = 1"},{"x":0.56921,"y":0.16394,"w":0.24239,"h":0.03019,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"e2, e1>2 = 2e, e2>3 = 2 3 e2,"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.20172,"w":0.59301,"h":0.02957,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"and so on. Since e is positive, er is positive too. Thus, er has a logarithm. When we take the logarithm, we find that for r rational"},{"x":0.53116,"y":0.23252,"w":0.17326,"h":0.02238,"fs":0.01918,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ln er = r ln e = r # 1 = r."},{"x":0.17313,"y":0.24591,"w":0.02761,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"(1, e)"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.25938,"w":0.5482,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Then applying the function ln-1 to both sides of the equation ln er = r, we find that"},{"x":0.25016,"y":0.26388,"w":0.04252,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"y = ln x"},{"x":0.47137,"y":0.2824,"w":0.44088,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"er = exp r for r rational. exp is ln-1. (5)"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.30869,"w":0.59306,"h":0.06071,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"We have not yet found a way to give an exact meaning to ex for x irrational. But ln-1x has meaning for any x, rational or irrational. So Equation (5) provides a way to extend the definition of ex to irrational values of x. The function expx is defined for all x, so we use it to assign a value to ex at every point."},{"x":0.067,"y":0.31418,"w":0.04768,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"−1 −2"},{"x":0.12942,"y":0.31533,"w":0.13179,"h":0.01024,"fs":0.01023,"n":5,"k":0,"t":"1 0 2 e 4"},{"x":0.04848,"y":0.38505,"w":0.24417,"h":0.02763,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 7.3 The graphs of y = lnx and y = ln-1x = expx. The number e is"},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.40273,"w":0.52842,"h":0.02882,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"DEFINITION For every real number x, we define the natural exponential func- tion to be ex = exp x."},{"x":0.0493,"y":0.41481,"w":0.1009,"h":0.01321,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ln-11 = exp (1)."},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.45869,"w":0.86786,"h":0.0565,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"For the first time we have a precise meaning for a number raised to an irrational power. Usually the exponential function is denoted by ex rather than exp x. Since ln x and ex are inverses of one another, we have Typical values of ex"},{"x":0.06648,"y":0.52396,"w":0.16401,"h":0.0143,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"x ex (rounded)"},{"x":0.35277,"y":0.53605,"w":0.22943,"h":0.0143,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Inverse Equations for ex and ln x"},{"x":0.05997,"y":0.54691,"w":0.11105,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"-1 0.37"},{"x":0.54611,"y":0.55544,"w":0.15814,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"elnx = x (all x 7 0)"},{"x":0.05405,"y":0.56655,"w":0.18587,"h":0.09093,"fs":0.01279,"n":10,"k":0,"t":"0 1 1 2.72 2 7.39 10 22026 100 2.6881 * 1043"},{"x":0.5305,"y":0.58219,"w":0.13995,"h":0.01422,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"ln(ex) = x (all x)"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.63246,"w":0.24692,"h":0.01504,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"The Derivative and Integral of ex"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.65822,"w":0.59302,"h":0.04348,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"The exponential function is differentiable because it is the inverse of a differentiable function whose derivative is never zero. We calculate its derivative using Theorem 3 of Section 3.8 and our knowledge of the derivative of ln x. Let"},{"x":0.45058,"y":0.70678,"w":0.33441,"h":0.01795,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"ƒ(x) = ln x and y = ex = ln-1x = ƒ -1(x)."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.73496,"w":0.03771,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Then,"},{"x":0.05994,"y":0.74867,"w":0.23797,"h":0.02962,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"The notations ln-1 x, exp x, and ex all refer to the natural exponential function."},{"x":0.46327,"y":0.75598,"w":0.05171,"h":0.02973,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"dy dx = d"},{"x":0.50318,"y":0.75844,"w":0.07676,"h":0.02728,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dx(ex) = d"},{"x":0.56814,"y":0.76377,"w":0.05206,"h":0.02194,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dxln-1x"},{"x":0.55025,"y":0.78913,"w":0.02886,"h":0.01999,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= d"},{"x":0.56732,"y":0.79446,"w":0.05841,"h":0.02194,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"dxƒ -1(x)"},{"x":0.55071,"y":0.81937,"w":0.08242,"h":0.02872,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"= 1 ƒ′(ƒ -1(x))"},{"x":0.66523,"y":0.82831,"w":0.1225,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Theorem 3, Section 3.8"},{"x":0.55071,"y":0.85326,"w":0.17664,"h":0.02744,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":0,"t":"= 1 ƒ′(ex) ƒ-1(x) = ex"},{"x":0.55071,"y":0.88523,"w":0.03779,"h":0.01914,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"= 1"},{"x":0.66605,"y":0.88803,"w":0.05105,"h":0.01637,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ƒ′(z) = 1"},{"x":0.71129,"y":0.89303,"w":0.07017,"h":0.01513,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"z with z = ex"},{"x":0.56942,"y":0.90016,"w":0.01909,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"a 1"},{"x":0.57838,"y":0.90016,"w":0.02101,"h":0.03007,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"exb"},{"x":0.55071,"y":0.93431,"w":0.03478,"h":0.01422,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"= ex."}]}